    "Subtitle": {},
    "Sufficient Contrast": {},
    "Suggest Answers…": {},
    "Suggest App Compliance answers for %@ in Atlas. Call get_compliance for what each regulation asks, the answers it takes and the trader details Atlas prepared. Read the project: whether the app makes money (paid app, subscriptions, in-app purchases, ads) for the Digital Services Act, and the company name, address, phone and email from its legal pages, imprint, terms or support docs; an ICP Filing Number in the app or its website files; for China mainland, whether it generates content with AI (chatbots, image or video generation, voice or face changes: AI SDKs, model APIs, on-device models), whose model it is and any CAC filing number and name in the project, and whether it has features that need a Security Assessment (sharing AI-generated content with other people, end-to-end encrypted messaging); and whether the app diagnoses, treats or monitors a medical condition, for the medical device question. Send them with propose_compliance_answers, each with why and evidence (file and line), and say in could_not_see what the project can't show. Never make up an address, a phone number, an ICP Filing Number or a filing number: leave out what you can't find. The person accepts each answer in Atlas and enters it in App Store Connect.": {},
    "Suggest App Information and App Review details for %@ in Atlas, from the code, and App Review's contact if it's empty.": {},
    "Suggest Everything": {},
    "Suggest Everything includes these too, so one prompt drafts every new page.": {},
    "Suggest Everything needs an assistant. Connect one here; when you’re done, it starts from Overview.": {},
    "Suggest Everything with %@": {},
    "Suggest Everything…": {},
    "Suggest German keywords for my app": {},
    "Suggest In-App Texts for %@ in Atlas: the subscription group's display name, and each subscription's and in-app purchase's display name and description, in every language of its Store Page where one is missing. Read them with get_in_app_texts, write in each language and within the limits, and propose them with propose_in_app_texts. Leave texts that are already there as they are, unless get_in_app_texts' states say App Review would reject one: propose a version without it. %@": {},
    "Suggest Pages…": {},
    "Suggest Prices…": {},
    "Suggest Screens": {},
    "Suggest Store Page or App Information values for the person to accept": {},
    "Suggest Store Page text for %@ in Atlas: What’s New, promotional text and subtitle, in %@ and in every language that has a translation.": {},
    "Suggest Texts…": {},
    "Suggest a Shorter Version": {},
    "Suggest a better subtitle for %@": {},
    "Suggest a better subtitle for %@ in its primary language with Atlas’s suggest_fields tool, read from the store page. Say in its reason that it’s a test sent from Settings. Propose nothing else.": {},
    "Suggest a page with propose_product_page: who it's for, its own evidence Atlas can show (those numbers, get_reviews, the approved keywords), keywords from keywords.<locale>.free that its audience searches (search results show the page's first three screenshots, not its text), promotional text in every language and the screens it leads with. Write promotional text for a page's languages with propose_product_page_texts. Don't suggest a dismissed suggestion again; send one of yours that's open again under its name to improve it. The person accepts, pushes and submits; you can't push, submit or make a page visible.": {},
    "Suggest and edit drafts": {},
    "Suggest better store texts for %@ in Atlas, in %@ only. Read get_listing and get_store_page for that language, get_reviews for the words people use, and this project for what the app really does. Suggest the subtitle, promotional text, description and keywords with suggest_fields, each with its reason and evidence. Leave the other languages: they're translated afterwards.": {},
    "Suggest custom product pages for %@ in Atlas. Don't push or submit anything.\n\n1. Read get_product_pages: the pages and their numbers against the default page, pageLimit, keywords (only keywords.<locale>.free can be chosen) and suggestions. Don't suggest a dismissed page again. To improve a page you suggested that's still open, send it again under the same name.\n2. Find who a page is for, from evidence: get_analytics_funnel; get_analytics_breakdown by source (a page reaches people through search, with the keywords it chooses, or through a link: ads, social posts, the links the app shares), by language and by territory (a large market whose view or download rate is worse than average deserves a page written for it), and by productPage once pages have numbers; get_reviews; get_in_app_events for the seasons ahead. Read this project for what the app really does, its screens, the links it shares and the deep links it opens.\n3. Suggest the 2 to 4 pages most likely to win downloads, best first, each for a different audience. Choose its keywords %@ by what its audience searches in each language: a keyword leads to one page, so leave the broad ones to the default page. Write its promotional text for that audience in every language the app has, in that language's own words. List the screens it should lead with, real ones from the project: search results show its first three screenshots, not its text. Set a deep link only when the project opens it. Give each page its own evidence, with the numbers that point to it.\n\nSend each with propose_product_page.": {},
    "Suggest every Store Page and App Information field, prices and App Privacy the code can answer": {},
    "Suggest everything Atlas can still change for %@ %@: %@. Read the project first. Skip fields I’ve already changed.": {},
    "Suggest everything for %@ in Atlas. Read the project first, then suggest every Store Page field in every language, including languages the app supports that aren’t on the App Store yet, and App Information: General, Age Rating, Accessibility for each device and its URL, App Privacy’s User Privacy Choices URL in each language, and App Review’s contact, sign-in and notes. Skip fields I’ve already changed.": {},
    "Suggest for %@ Only…": {},
    "Suggest for Store Page": {},
    "Suggest for the Primary Language Only…": {},
    "Suggest new Apple Watch screens for %@’s screenshot plan in Atlas.": {},
    "Suggest new screens for %@’s screenshot plan in Atlas.": {},
    "Suggest only": {},
    "Suggest prices for %@'s subscriptions in Atlas. Read get_subscriptions, get_prices and get_price_points, and get_analytics_subscriptions and get_analytics_breakdown by territory for where people convert and where they don't. Suggest each territory's price with propose_prices, from Apple's price points only, with the evidence for each: what people there pay for apps like this, and the numbers. Never quote a rate marked tooFewToTell.": {},
    "Suggest replies to %@’s App Store reviews in Atlas. Read them with get_reviews. For each review that needs a reply, write one in the reviewer’s own language, short and specific to what they wrote, and send it with propose_review_replies together with what it means in English. Read the project to say what is fixed or planned, and never promise what isn’t there. %@ Then group the reviews by subject with propose_review_themes: what’s broken, what people ask for, what they love, each with one action and, for reply_to_all, one message per language. Nothing reaches the App Store from you: the person sends each reply.": {},
    "Suggest the App Compliance answers the project can show": {},
    "Suggest the texts of %@’s In-App Events in Atlas. Read them with get_in_app_events: each event’s name (30 characters), short description (50) and long description (120) in its primary language, and the languages still missing. For every missing language write all three in that language, natural rather than literal (a winter event isn’t one where December is summer), in the voice of that language’s store page, within the limits, and send them with propose_event_texts with one sentence on why they read this way. Leave the languages that are written. %@": {},
