    "The person already queued dates of their own (personsDates): those are kept. Yours wait beside them as a suggestion, on the event’s page under Details and in Review & Push, where the person accepts yours (they replace theirs) or dismisses them (theirs stay). Sending dates again replaces your suggestion.": {},
    "The person asked Atlas for the artwork of %@’s In-App Event “%@” (app %@, event %@): %lld concepts from Claude, each the event card (16:9) and the event details image (9:16) as a matching pair. Work in this order.\n1. Read get_event_artwork_brief: the brief in the person’s words (it comes first), the look to start from, the options, the event’s texts, the app’s design, the art direction and the rules. If its design says the look is missing, read it in the project and send it with report_app_support.\n2. Find the app’s visual world with Glob, Grep and Read: the look’s designSource files and the code that draws its scenes, backgrounds or illustrations, above all any that fit this event. The art should look like it comes from the app.\n3. Choose %lld different ideas about this event, then make each image one full HTML page with CSS and inline SVG at the canvas size, crafted as the art direction says.\n4. Render each card and details page with render_event_artwork, without motion, and look at both images it returns: the art, and the art under the App Store’s text. Check them against the art direction’s selfCheck, fix what fails and render again.%@\n5. Send each concept with propose_event_artwork_concept, one call each, marking the one you’d choose with pick. Fix every refusal and send it again.\nNever push and never submit the event: the person chooses a concept in Atlas.": {},
    "The person asked Atlas for the custom product page “%@” of %@ (app %@) to have its own screenshots: the default set’s screens in another order, with captions written for this page’s audience, in %lld languages. Work in this order.\n1. Read get_page_screenshots with page “%@”: who the page is for, its brief, keywords and promotional text, the default set’s screens (their captures are attached) and their captions, the languages and the rules.\n2. Look in the project with Glob, Grep and Read for what each screen does for this audience, so the captions say it in the app’s own words.\n3. Choose the order that tells this page’s story (its first three show in search results) and write a caption for each screen in the primary language, then in the others: translated for meaning, as short.\n4. Send it with propose_page_screenshots: the primary language first, then the other languages a few at a time with the same screens. Fix every caption it lists in `refused` and send it again.\nNever push and never submit the page: the person looks at the plan in Atlas and makes the set.": {},
    "The person asked for a change to the concept “%@” of %@’s In-App Event “%@” (app %@, event %@). Read get_event_artwork_brief: its revision has what they asked for, the concept’s pages and its images. Redraw only that concept, keeping what they didn’t ask to change. Render it with render_event_artwork and look at it under the App Store’s text before you send it with propose_event_artwork_concept and replaces. Never push and never submit the event.": {},
    "The person asked for changes to this concept: send them with revise_artwork_concept.": {},
    "The person asked for changes: send them with revise_direction.": {},
    "The person asked for some screens of the custom product page “%@” of %@ (app %@) to be captured again, so they show what this page’s audience came for. Work in this order.\n1. Read get_page_screenshots with page “%@”: the page, the plan, the default set’s screens (their captures are attached) and `recapture`, with its upload link and the devices and languages it needs.\n2. Choose at most %lld screens whose capture should show something else for this audience (the lock turned on, the kind of entries this page is about). Leave the others as they are.\n3. Look for the project’s screenshot UI tests first (Grep for XCUIScreen or screenshot in its UI test files): they reach the screens that need taps and set up sample data. Run the ones for your screens with xcodebuild test -only-testing:… -resultBundlePath <path>, with the values they read (a topic, an entry) given after xcodebuild as build settings, NAME=value (their comments or the test scheme’s environment say which; nothing goes in front of xcodebuild), and export the captures with xcrun xcresulttool export attachments. Without such tests, build the app and run it in the Simulator with the app’s launch arguments, then save the screen with xcrun simctl io booted screenshot. Use %@ for iPhone, %@ for iPad, turned the way `recapture` says (its devices’ orientation and pixel size), in each language the set is made in (-testLanguage and -testRegion, or -AppleLanguages and -AppleLocale).\n4. Send each PNG with curl -s --data-binary @file.png -H 'Content-Type: image/png' <uploadURL>, the link as is (not in quotes), and name it with report_page_capture: the screen’s number, the language, the device, the upload ID and what it shows now.\nNever push and never submit the page, and don’t change the app’s code: the person makes the set in Atlas.": {},
    "The person asked: “%@”. Change only that, and send the whole plan again with propose_page_screenshots; Atlas lays the set out again.": {},
    "The person asks for: “%@”.": {},
    "The person changed it in Atlas and hasn’t pushed it yet.": {},
    "The person chose %@ (%@): animate that one.": {},
